.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_REG_GENERATION 3 "2026-07-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation \- caller-visible identity token for a registration's fd
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

uint64_t ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a monotonically increasing value, unique loop-wide, minted once
when the fd (or queue/ccol_channel bridge) reg belongs to is first
registered (ccol_event_loop_add()'s new-entry path) and shared by every
registration on that same fd for as long as it lives; including
across ccol_event_loop_modify() (a direction flip is the same underlying
fd/connection, so it keeps the same generation) and across both a read
and a write registration on the same fd (both share one generation,
since they represent one logical connection).

This exists for a caller's own defensive bookkeeping across fd reuse:
a caller holding onto a connection object across several asynchronous
steps can stamp it with the generation it read right after
ccol_event_loop_add() returned, and later compare against a fresh read to
detect whether it is still reasoning about the same logical
connection, or whether the underlying fd number has since been closed
and reused by an unrelated new connection.

Generation is not required for basic correctness. ccol_event_loop's own
dispatch path already validates a registration's liveness before
invoking any callback, unconditionally, whether or not a caller ever
calls this function at all: with more than one reactor thread, the
single polling thread collects readiness and a separate dispatch
worker thread runs the callback afterward, so a registration removed
(or whose fd was reused by a brand-new registration) between one such
batch being collected and processed is safely skipped at dispatch
time rather than misdispatched or leading to a use-after-free.  This
function's return value is purely for the calling application's own
bookkeeping on top of that guarantee.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop reg belongs to.
reg   Registration to query.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the generation value.
Returns 0 if reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID or otherwise fails to resolve
(including because it was already removed), or if loop is
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle (0 is never a
valid generation for a real registration; the counter starts at 1).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call at any time on any ccol_event_reg value ever returned by
ccol_event_loop_add for this loop, including one already removed (whether
by this thread earlier or a genuinely concurrent one racing right
now): reg is resolved against loop's own registration table before
anything is dereferenced, so an already-removed or otherwise stale
handle is always detected as such and reported as generation 0, never
a use-after-free.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread, at any time, including concurrently with
dispatch, ccol_event_loop_modify(), and ccol_event_loop_remove().
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct conn {
    ccol_event_reg reg;
    uint64_t generation;
} conn_t;

conn_t *c = malloc(sizeof(*c));
c->reg = ccol_event_loop_add(loop, ccol_selectable_from_fd(fd, ccol_select_read),
                        handlers, c, NULL);
c->generation = ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(loop, c->reg);

/* ... later, from some other asynchronous step holding a conn_t* ... */
if (ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(loop, c->reg) != c->generation) {
    /* stale: this fd number has been closed and reused since */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_modify (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3)
